WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:06.640
You’re chasing your paper boat as it floats
down the rain-smattered rivulets running along

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.670
the curb by your house, hurrying faster and
faster as the stream turns into a mini-river...

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.000
In most writers’ hands, you have a reasonable
idea of what to expect next:

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.170
But not in the hands of Stephen King.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.020
Now yes, sure, when you say “Stephen King,”
your average Joe Schmo thinks “Boo!

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.020
Scary!”

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.430
And hey, that’s just fine with Uncle Stevie.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:29.690
Fellow novelist John Grisham says King “has
described him as the current holder of ABLB,

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.730
or America’s Best-Loved Bogeyman.”

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.730
And that paper boat?

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.980
She’s forever lost in the sewer, held hostage
by Pennywise the Clown.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.980
And Pennywise doesn’t make balloon animals
or honk his nose.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:41.980
Nope.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:42.980
Nosiree.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.950
He eats children just… like…

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:45.950
YOU.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.160
Few writers have had the sheer staying power,
popularity, and prolific output as Stephen

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.160
King.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.690
From insatiably flesh-hungry clowns and sentient
cars, to telekinetic teenagers and mystical

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.970
gunslingers, if there’s one author who has
taken up valuable real estate in that part

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.739
of our imaginations, it’s Stephen King.

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.770
But it’s not just his monsters that have
lasting power—it’s also the very human

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.139
and very psychological elements in his work that linger.

00:01:11.139 --> 00:01:15.060
So come with me, Constant Reader, while I lead you through the dark and twisted world

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.979
of Uncle Stevie, the King of Horror…

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.249
[INTRO]

00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:24.929
Stephen King has been around a while, and when I say “a while,” I’m talking way

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:27.049
back to bell-bottoms and Watergate.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:31.499
Even before he published his first novel Carrie in 1974, he had published dozens of short

00:01:31.499 --> 00:01:32.499
stories.

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:35.890
To date, King’s books have sold more than
350 million copies.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:41.409
He has published a staggering 61 novels, 11 collections of short stories and novellas,

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:42.689
and 5 works of nonfiction.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:48.189
To say nothing of his screenplays, essays,
and yes, even a musical with John Mellencamp

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:53.630
and T Bone Burnett called Ghost Brothers of Darkland County.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.569
But when King is actually writing (which,
when he’s in the middle of a project, is

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:00.679
every day, including Christmas, the Fourth
of July, and his own birthday), he’s writing

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:01.939
a lot.

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:05.270
Any avid King reader knows that when they
pick up one of his books, they can generally

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:11.340
expect a doorstop.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.420
His longest, the unabridged version of The
Stand, clocks in at 1153 pages and was originally

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:22.650
so long that the publisher requested he cut
four hundred pages to lower production costs.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.280
Says King “I was asked if I would like

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.110
to make the cuts [to The Stand], or if I would prefer someone in the editorial department

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.110
to do it.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.740
I reluctantly agreed to do the surgery myself.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.790
I think I did a fairly good job, for a writer
who has been accused over and over again of

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.070
having diarrhea of the word processor.”

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:45.290
In other words, as the verbose author uncharacteristically
put it succinctly: “I have a real problem

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.190
with bloat—I write like fat ladies diet.”

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.730
Yike

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.570
So just how does King maintain such a consistent
output over the course of almost five decades?

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.150
Well, remember that aforementioned strict
work ethic that glues King to his keyboard

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.960
even on his own birthday?

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:01.960
According to King:

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.210
I like to get ten pages a day, which amounts
to 2,000 words.

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:08.980
That’s 180,000 words over a three-month
span, a goodish length for a book.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.670
. . The first draft of a book—even a long
one—should take no more than three months,

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:13.670
the length of a season.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.920
But King is the first to admit that he’s
been slowing down a bit.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.510
according to him: “I think it was quitting
smoking that slowed me down; nicotine is a

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.040
great synapse enhancer.”

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.370
And here, Dear Reader, is where his tale takes a dark path—the relentless demon of addiction

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.880
that not only plagued so much of his life,
but also informed a lot of his work.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.900
King has always been open about the dark periods in his life that have influenced his writing,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.069
for both good and ill.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.490
For example, Cujo, a novel about a mother
and her young son stranded in a stifling car

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:45.670
surrounded by a rabid St. Bernard, is a novel King himself likes.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.830
But sadly, he was so drunk that he barely
remembers writing it: “I was drinking a

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.630
case of sixteen-ounce tallboys a night.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.090
. . I don’t say that with pride or shame,
only with a vague sense of sorrow and loss.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.380
I like that book.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.750
I wish I could remember enjoying the good
parts as I put them down on the page.”

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:06.550
On the opposite end of the spectrum is his
alien invasion opus The Tommyknockers, which

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.160
King claims “is an awful book.”

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.350
When he wrote it in the spring and summer
of 1986, he was “often working until midnight

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.639
with my heart running at a hundred and thirty
beats a minute and cotton swabs stuck up my

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:20.310
nose to stem the coke-induced bleeding…

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.479
That [The Tommyknockers] was the last one
I wrote before I cleaned up my act.”

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:27.779
While King did a lot of hard work in the 90’s getting clean, a freak car accident in 1999

00:04:27.779 --> 00:04:32.099
while he was taking one of his nightly walks nearly ended his life, and some time after,

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:34.499
an addiction to painkillers entered back into the narrative.

00:04:34.499 --> 00:04:39.340
His first book while recovering, an alien
invasion called Dreamcatcher, was the freakish

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.210
offspring of severe pain and heavy doses of Oxycontin.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:46.990
The novel delves deep into the invaders (nicknamed
“Shit Weasels”) that gestate in their

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:51.189
hosts’ stomachs, only to violently and gruesomely burst from their rectums.

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:56.020
Dreamcatcher is also a book King is not a
fan of: “I don’t like Dreamcatcher very

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.020
much…

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.939
I was pretty stoned when I wrote it, because
of the Oxy, and that’s another book that

00:04:59.939 --> 00:05:01.719
shows the drugs at work.”

00:05:01.719 --> 00:05:04.199
Addiction is a pervasive theme in many King texts.

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:08.620
The Shining, about a troubled father who gives into his demons, both of the ghostly and bottled

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:13.009
variety, is a (447-page) metaphor for alcoholism.

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:16.539
And King admits that there was a part of him that knew he was an alcoholic while he was

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:19.840
writing it, even though he couldn’t admit
it at the time.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:20.840
And Misery?

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:21.840
Wait...

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.250
I thought that was about a crazy fan so obsessed
with an author and his work that she’s willing

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:27.090
to kill him to squeeze one final book out
of him?

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.509
Nope: “Misery is a book about cocaine.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:31.180
Annie Wilkes is cocaine.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.680
She was my number-one fan.”

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.400
And this is what makes Stephen King so successful—he
uses surface-level fears to examine the things

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.749
that truly horrify us.

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:42.689
It’s not that there are literal monsters
and demons that stalk us day to day, it’s

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:45.500
that King finds metaphorical ways to personify them.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.830
As fellow novelist Walter Mosley puts it:

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.840
King’s phenomenal popularity is due to his
almost instinctual understanding of the fears

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.639
that form the psyche of America’s working
class.

00:05:54.639 --> 00:05:55.780
He knows fear.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:00.500
And not the fear of demonic forces alone,
but also of loneliness and poverty, of hunger

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.460
and the unknown we have to breach in order to survive.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:03.460
This is why Stephen King has had such immense staying power; Because the true monster lurking

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.460
beneath all his spooky stuff is trauma.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:08.089
Trauma is always there, an omnipresent part
of the human experience: the trauma of helplessness

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:09.592
and loss of agency (Misery, Gerald’s Game,
Rita Hayworth and the Shawshank Redemption);

00:06:09.592 --> 00:06:12.260
the trauma of abuse and fear of your own loved
ones (The Shining, Carrie, Rose Madder); and

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:15.159
the biggest one of all: childhood trauma (The
Body, Apt Pupil, Hearts in Atlantis, Dreamcatcher,

00:06:15.159 --> 00:06:16.199
The Shining again, Carrie again, Cujo, The
Institute, Firestarter, The Talisman, The

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:17.229
Girl Who Loved Tom Gordon, and the granddaddy
of them all: It).

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:19.309
He’s done that one… a few times.

00:06:19.309 --> 00:06:24.439
I mean, the creature from It literally takes
the form of whatever will be most traumatizing

00:06:24.439 --> 00:06:28.520
to each kid, ensuring that each and every
one of them will have one hell of a time growing

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.520
up.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.339
This is all a long-winded way of saying, as
Mr. Diarrhea of the Word Processor put it:

00:06:33.339 --> 00:06:37.460
“You don’t get scared of monsters; you
get scared for people.”

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.960
This ability to grapple with the deeper, more
profound horrors that pervade the human condition

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.430
has allowed King to branch out into just about
every genre, making him a slippery writer

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:47.919
to pin down, genre-wise.

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:52.420
But to reductively label King as just a “Horror
Writer” is like saying Tolkien is just a

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.300
guy who writes about jewelry.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.789
For every child-killing clown King has given
us, he’s also given us a tender love story

00:06:58.789 --> 00:06:59.909
about loss like (Lisey’s Story, King’s
personal favorite).

00:06:59.909 --> 00:07:04.319
And for every rabid St. Bernard, we get an
inspirational story of redemption from a maximum

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:06.669
security prison, as in The Green Mile.

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:11.969
Literary critic Michael R. Collings calls
this “generic indecisiveness,” which English

00:07:11.969 --> 00:07:17.119
lecturer John Sears observes is a hallmark
of King’s works, as they demonstrate “an

00:07:17.119 --> 00:07:21.559
unwillingness or inability to be confined
to singular generic categories.

00:07:21.559 --> 00:07:25.999
They tend instead to fray at their generic
edges, sprawl over boundaries, allowing different

00:07:25.999 --> 00:07:27.900
genres to seep into each other.”

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:33.099
This refusal to be confined to one genre is
no more abundantly clear than in King’s

00:07:33.099 --> 00:07:38.229
sprawling Dark Tower series, eight volumes spanning thirty years of his career, and whose

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:44.409
characters, ideas, and world make cameos in many of King’s other non-Dark Tower works.

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:47.529
If you’ve read The Dark Tower, try describing
it.

00:07:47.529 --> 00:07:48.529
Just try.

00:07:48.529 --> 00:07:51.159
And no, you’re not allowed to cheat and
give me that Matthew McConaughy movie.

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:56.130
It’s only fitting to end our examination
of a master storyteller with a story, this

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:58.240
one paraphrased from King himself...

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.689
Way back after Carrie debuted, King and his
editor were waiting at a crosswalk trying

00:08:02.689 --> 00:08:07.309
to decide which book King would publish next:
Roadwork or ‘Salem’s Lot.

00:08:07.309 --> 00:08:11.379
There were pros and cons to each, but his
editor’s main concern was that if they went

00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:15.409
with ‘Salem’s Lot, King would most assuredly
be typed as a horror writer.

00:08:15.409 --> 00:08:19.419
King said, “I don’t care what they call
me as long as the checks don’t bounce.”

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:23.060
They went with ‘Salem’s Lot, the modern
American vampire bestseller and the checks

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:24.360
certainly didn’t bounce.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.590
And the result?

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.590
Well...

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:30.749
“I was indeed typed as a horror writer,
a tag I have never confirmed or denied, simply

00:08:30.749 --> 00:08:33.130
because I think it’s irrelevant to what
I do.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.060
It does, however, give bookstores a handy
place to shelve my books.”

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:41.340
King’s work is so expansive, so sprawling,
and so exhaustively wide and deep, that we

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.169
could spend a whole YouTube channel expanding
on it.

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.630
But at the end of the day, we need some place
to put his books.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:58.370
Just make sure the shelves are a safe distance
from your bed, or else get too close, or Uncle

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:05.730
Stevie will make his nightmares your own.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:09.370
And there’s nothing like sharing a nightmare
with Stephen King.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:15.120
Goodnight... and sweet dreams, Constant Reader...

